#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""orca-slice: slice a model with Jonas's OrcaSlicer fork and his SimpleCore presets.

Replaces orcaweb (the old :8080 phone slicer) for agents. Model in, G-code out.
It never uploads to the printer or starts a print: send the G-code to Moonraker
yourself, and only after Jonas says go.

Examples:
  orca-slice part.stl                                  # SimpleCore / Parts 0.2 / ABS Sunlu
  orca-slice part.stl -P "SimpleCore Parts 0.25" -f "SimpleCore ASA" -o out/
  orca-slice part.stl --support tree --set wall_loops=4 --set sparse_infill_density=30%
  orca-slice part.stl --lane 3                          # Trad Rack lane 3: T0 -> T2
  orca-slice painted.3mf --as-is                        # multi-colour 3MF exactly as saved
  orca-slice part.stl --html                            # also write part.html, a toolpath preview
  orca-slice --list                                     # list printer/process/filament names

Settings (--set routes every key to where it lives):
  orca-slice part.stl --set nozzle_temperature=250      # filament key: every filament
  orca-slice part.stl -f A -f B --set 'nozzle_temperature[2]=250'   # filament 2 only
  orca-slice part.stl --set 'retraction_length[1]=0.8'  # printer key, extruder 1
  orca-slice part.stl --set-object wall_loops=6         # per object (the GUI's object settings)
  orca-slice part.stl --set-range 0-3:sparse_infill_density=100%   # height range modifier
  orca-slice part.stl --modifier box@10,10,5:20,20,10:sparse_infill_density=80%,wall_loops=5
  orca-slice part.stl --negative cylinder@0,0,0:3,40    # subtract a 3 mm radius, 40 mm cylinder
  orca-slice part.stl --cut 12:lower --copies 4         # keep the part below z=12, four copies
  orca-slice job.json   or   orca-slice job job.json    # full job file (objects, parts, paint, plates)
  orca-slice options --search pressure                  # setting catalog (JSON)

Transforms and exports:
  orca-slice part.stl --orient                          # auto-orient (default: keep as modelled)
  orca-slice part.stl --rotate-x 90 --lay-flat --scale 1.02
  orca-slice part.stl --export-3mf part.3mf             # GUI-openable project with every setting
  orca-slice part.stl --no-slice --export-stls out/     # arrange/transform only, write STLs
  orca-slice part.stl --stats --strict                  # statistics JSON; fail on slicing warnings

Printer and hub (read only unless you push; tokens come from the environment only):
  orca-slice lanes                                      # what Trad Rack holds (Moonraker lane_data)
  orca-slice part.stl -f @T7                            # the preset loaded in T7, retooled to T7
  orca-slice part.stl -f @printer                       # every lane, T0..Tn, in order
  orca-slice calib temp_tower --params start=270,end=240,step=5
  FILAMENT_HUB_TOKEN=... orca-slice hub pull            # hub presets -> ~/.cache/orca-slice/hub
  FILAMENT_HUB_TOKEN=... orca-slice hub push "SimpleCore ASA" --type filament

Presets are read from ~/.config/OrcaSlicer/user/default (the desktop fork's user
presets), then from ~/.cache/orca-slice/hub (`orca-slice hub pull`), and resolved
against the fork's system profiles, then flattened into self-contained JSON (same
logic orcaweb used: missing parents fall back to the system generic,
process<->printer compat is patched, bed temp forced non-zero).
Brims are always forced off (brim_type=no_brim) unless you --set brim_type=...

--set KEY=VALUE writes a process key into the process preset, a filament key into
every filament (KEY[i]=VALUE: filament i only, 1-based), a printer key into the
printer preset (KEY[i]=VALUE: extruder i), and passes project keys to the slicer
as --key value. Unknown keys and enum values the slicer would not accept are
errors, as is any value the slicer replaced while loading (result.json warning
config_value_substituted) unless --allow-substitutions is given. Routing needs
the fork binary's --list-options; older binaries fall back to "key found in the
printer/filament preset goes there, else process".

Per-object options (--set-object, --set-range, --modifier, --negative, --cut,
--copies) build a job file around the model and slice it with --load-job.
--modifier/--negative take SHAPE@X,Y,Z:SIZE[:KEY=VALUE,...] with SHAPE box (SIZE
sx,sy,sz), cylinder or cone (r,h) or sphere (r); X,Y,Z is the shape's centre in
the model file's own coordinates. Job files are described in the fork's
docs/headless-cli.md.

--html asks the fork CLI for --export-gcode-html and puts the page next to the
G-code (same base name, .html): one self-contained file with a WebGL viewer
(layer/move sliders, colour modes, feature legend) that opens offline in any
browser. The JSON output then carries its path as "html".

Several plates: "gcode" is plate 1, and "plates" lists every plate as
<name>_plate<N>.gcode with its own stats; --plate N slices plate N only.

Toolhead clearance (fork, printer setting toolhead_clearance_enable=1, off by default):
the fork scans the finished G-code against the part printed so far and writes
<name>.clearance.json next to it; the JSON output carries it as "clearance"
(collisions, min_gap_mm, first colliding lines). Collisions also warn on stderr.

Subcommands: options, inspect, calib, job, lanes, hub (orca-slice SUBCOMMAND --help).
Environment: ORCA_DIR (fork package dir), MOONRAKER_URL, MOONRAKER_API_KEY,
FILAMENT_HUB_URL, FILAMENT_HUB_TOKEN.
"""
